# Line endings are part of this repository's CONTRACT for three files, not a cosmetic preference.
#
# tools/generate_distribution_artifacts.py compares THIRD_PARTY_NOTICES.md and SOURCE_OFFER.md
# BYTE FOR BYTE, and the notice embeds the SHA-256 of Cargo.lock. The first Windows release leg
# failed on exactly that: git on the Windows runner image checks out with core.autocrlf, every
# LF became CRLF, and the check reported "stale THIRD_PARTY_NOTICES.md; stale SOURCE_OFFER.md"
# for files whose content was correct. Reproduced locally by converting those three files to
# CRLF, which printed the identical message.
#
# `-text` and not `* text=auto eol=lf`. The broad form was written first and rejected on
# measurement: THIRD_PARTY_NOTICES.md legitimately contains 23 CRLF sequences, embedded from
# vendored licence text, and `text=auto` would normalise them away the next time the file is
# staged -- silently changing the bytes of the very artifact whose bytes are the contract.
# `-text` tells git to convert these files in NEITHER direction, which is the actual requirement.
THIRD_PARTY_NOTICES.md -text
SOURCE_OFFER.md -text
Cargo.lock -text

# Vendored upstream text is compared against its pinned origin and hashed by the drift harness,
# so it has the same requirement for the same reason.
third_party/** -text

# Binary assets must never be touched. Listed explicitly rather than left to auto-detection,
# because a corrupted icon is not a build failure -- it renders as a blank frame.
*.png binary
*.jpg binary
*.jpeg binary
*.ico binary
*.icns binary
*.gz binary
*.zip binary
*.7z binary
*.abr binary
*.gbr binary
*.gih binary
*.icc binary
*.ttf binary
*.otf binary
*.woff binary
*.woff2 binary
